    {
      "title": "OpenAI unveils 'always-on' AI agents as company pauses model rollout",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/technology/artificial-intelligence/openai-unveils-always-on-ai-agents-as-company-pauses-model-rollout-126093000057_1.html",
      "description": "OpenAI CEO Sam Altman introduced a \"remarkably capable, always-on\" artificial intelligence agent at an appearance Tuesday, a day after the company halted the rollout of a more advanced model over security concerns.\nAt the company's annual developer conference, Altman made a slew of product announcements and updates, including OpenAI's new agents, called Dots. The agents, designed to complete ongoing tasks proactively on behalf of users, are a competitor of Meta's personal agent Muse, which exploded in popularity after Meta's own conference last week.\nDot agents will be \"like an AI helper that always has your back, inspired by the cool versions of what we all watched in movies growing up,\" Altman said.\nOn Monday, OpenAI said it was holding off on releasing the other model because of concerns raised by its researchers. That followed broader calls within the industry to decelerate the pace of the technology's advance to let safety measures catch up.\nAltman avoided making references to",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-09-30T03:08:48"
    }
  ]
